# Platform Contract

Flutter feature-first · BLoC/Cubit · GetIt · Supabase · Hive · Firebase Messaging.
Full contract in [`AGENTS.md`](../../../AGENTS.md); architecture in [`docs/architecture-overview.md`](../../../docs/architecture-overview.md).

## Purpose

Keep every change on the approved stack and inside the approved boundaries so the SaaS template stays cloneable.

## When Applied

Always. This is the baseline every agent and edit inherits.

## Requirements

- Stack is fixed: BLoC/Cubit + GetIt + Supabase + Hive + FCM.
- Remote I/O lives only in repositories/datasources — never widgets/pages.
- Multi-step DB work uses Supabase **RPCs**.
- Preserve cache-first / stale-while-revalidate for lists and dashboards.
- Register repos/datasources as lazy singletons, BLoCs/Cubits as factories, in `lib/core/di/injection.dart`.
- Authenticated member/admin UI enters only through `AuthenticatedShell`.
- Hand-written `fromJson`/`fromMap` models; `supabase/*.sql` is schema source of truth.
- Never place `service_role` or secrets in the Flutter client.
- When unsure, copy the gold standards: `lib/features/auth` (boundaries), `lib/features/booking` + `lib/features/sessions` (cache + realtime).

## Examples

- New live admin list → extend `StreamRepository<T>`, register lazy singleton, expose via BLoC.
- New table write that spans rows → add a `SECURITY DEFINER` RPC and call `.rpc(...)`.

## Anti-patterns

- Adding Provider, Riverpod, GetX, injectable codegen, Freezed, Dio, or a new router package.
- Calling `Supabase.instance` from a widget "just this once".

## Common Mistakes

- Inventing a new top-level pattern instead of extending an existing feature.
- Skipping DI registration and constructing repositories inline.
